(THE AWAKENING OF THE CRUISER) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                              _From Paris to Toulon, end of July, 1914._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From the corridor I watch through the windows the swift receding of) Tj T*
(Paris. In this express-train, the last to run according to the normal) Tj T*
(schedule, are numerous naval officers en route for Toulon. Some have) Tj T*
(broken their brief vacations; almost all are returning on leaves of) Tj T*
(absence from their studies. The call of our country sends us towards) Tj T*
(the sea, that field of battle which we have chosen. To the French Navy) Tj T*
(belongs the ?honor? of the Mediterranean, and our fleet is at its) Tj T*
(summit of preparedness. We know that the decisive duel will be fought) Tj T*
(in the fields of Flanders or on the slopes of the Vosges. But our) Tj T*
(effort will not be useless. We have only one fear--that we shall arrive) Tj T*
(too late, and miss that battle which our imaginations have pictured) Tj T*
(without actually believing.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dijon, Lyons, Valence, Marseilles. I have just left a Paris full of) Tj T*
(excitement, where life is of so poignant a sweetness that the people) Tj T*
(are eager to defend the happiness they possess in such abundance. I am) Tj T*
(traveling through our smiling France. How many times, as I have passed) Tj T*
(from one seaport to another, on my way from a Chinese to an Atlantic) Tj T*
(cruise, have I not understood the envy which is directed towards her!) Tj T*
(How could our neighbors help casting towards this delightful land the) Tj T*
(glances of beasts of prey! Now they have spread out their claws, and) Tj T*
(hurled at her a cry of war. France has drawn herself erect. Everywhere) Tj T*
(squads of sentinels are guarding the roadways, the crossings, the) Tj T*
(stations, all the nerve-centers of mobilization. Into the eyes of the) Tj T*
(French people these last few days has come a magnificent expression; a) Tj T*
(new visage, which our race has put on as if for a fête, gives a family) Tj T*
(likeness to all its members. The foster-mother of children like these) Tj T*
(is no moribund being such as the Germans think they will succeed in) Tj T*
(doing away with. She has just felt again the vivid sense of her duty,) Tj T*
(and the heirs of her wonderful past draw from her strength attitudes so) Tj T*
(natural that they are not even astonished at them. This astonishment) Tj T*
(they leave to the rest of the world.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dijon, Lyons, Valence, Marseilles, Naguére. I amuse myself with) Tj T*
(observing the various types and accents of the provinces. To-day) Tj T*
(everyone speaks the same language, has the same expression; in every) Tj T*
(breast is the same heart. I know that in the West, in the regions I) Tj T*
(have not traveled through, Gascons, Normans, and Picards are feeling) Tj T*
(and acting alike. Among these troops assembled on the platforms of the) Tj T*
(stations, in the sleeping cottages of remote countrysides, in the towns) Tj T*
(past which glides our flashing train, there is only one dream. This) Tj T*
(dream I know, for it is my own:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?What post does France give me for the great combat? Wherever it may) Tj T*
(be, whether I fall, or whether I survive, it will be well.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
