BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
() Tj T*
(?You are in luck,? he says. ?All the officers who arrive at the port) Tj T*
(ask for the _Waldeck-Rousseau_.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(He guesses the question I dare not utter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?The engineers are counting on six weeks.... Let us hope nothing) Tj T*
(decisive happens at sea ... in case events are so precipitate....?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thinking over these words, I return to the gate of the arsenal. It is) Tj T*
(getting on towards five o?clock. The flame and shimmer of the afternoon) Tj T*
(light are marvelous. The Pharon, a mirror of stone, reflects the) Tj T*
(dazzling violet rays. It is the hottest part of the day. After this) Tj T*
(will follow cooling breezes. In front of the Missiessy gate mothers and) Tj T*
(wives crouch on the sidewalk, awaiting their sailors, who come out of) Tj T*
(the arsenal raising clouds of dust. A beverage-vender calls his wares) Tj T*
(in a nasal voice; several barkers offer for ten centimes a hundred) Tj T*
(attractions in the way of café-concerts; the tramcars, caparisoned in) Tj T*
(dust, go by in a torrid blast. It is so warm, the boulevard is so) Tj T*
(torpid, that I cannot think, and have but one swift desire--to change) Tj T*
(my stifling uniform for a more comfortable suit, and on a terrace to) Tj T*
(sip some cooling drink.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Suddenly, smothered by the distance and the heavy air, a dull cannon) Tj T*
(shot strikes into the fringe of my reverie. I fear I have heard) Tj T*
(amiss. I wait motionless, my whole body concentrated in my hearing.) Tj T*
(The boulevard seems petrified. With a brusque jamming on of brakes,) Tj T*
(the tramcars grind along the track; the windows bristle with anxious) Tj T*
(faces. The women squatting on the sidewalk silently rise; barkers) Tj T*
(and passers-by forget to live; everyone, in the posture in which the) Tj T*
(vague shot has surprised him, listens to the dramatic silence. All the) Tj T*
(noises of the city, the deepest as well as the shrillest, vanish into) Tj T*
(nothingness to leave room for the one sound that has significance. In a) Tj T*
(sort of religious atmosphere the second shot booms and rolls, sonorous,) Tj T*
(the master of Space.... At length the third dies away, the third voice) Tj T*
(of a France who is placing herself on guard.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At the same time, over the deserted roadway, the trumpets sound from) Tj T*
(the barracks. Listen to those majestic singing tones, which bring tears) Tj T*
(to the driest eyelids! It is the call of France! Drawn up under the) Tj T*
(great trees a whole wan city salutes two little soldiers who swell) Tj T*
(their cheeks upon the shining trumpets. They are much affected, these) Tj T*
(two little soldiers in fatigue uniform; their step is hesitant, and) Tj T*
(their breath breaks. But their eyes are sparkling, each measure brings) Tj T*
(new vigor to their step, they find the theme again, and without taking) Tj T*
(breath they sound the ?générale? out to the suburbs, to the slopes of) Tj T*
(the Pharon, to the roads of the countryside roundabout. They are the) Tj T*
(heralds of their country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At this instant all over this land the same trumpet is being blown. It) Tj T*
(has found me in a warm and fragrant province, but everywhere millions) Tj T*
(of reapers, with suspended sickles, are listening to the same notes) Tj T*
ET
