BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(flung out over oceans of grain. Mountains and valleys give back its) Tj T*
(echo to the huts of cattlemen and shepherds, and the silent waters of) Tj T*
(the rivers quiver as they receive its melody. For the first time in the) Tj T*
(course of the centuries the race of France is listening at the same) Tj T*
(instant to a voice which orders her to face towards a common point.) Tj T*
(Stirred by a great hope, her hearts are celebrating together this first) Tj T*
(communion of heroism.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Fortune compels me to wait six weeks before playing my rôle. My weapon) Tj T*
(of war is not yet ready. I can only admire, as a spectator, deeds in) Tj T*
(which I have no share.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the streets leading to the harbor, the heart of Toulon, swarm crowds) Tj T*
(of people. I am not acquainted with these figures that slip along) Tj T*
(beside me, but I recognize them all. Marines from Brittany, blue-eyed,) Tj T*
(with swinging step, white-coiffed wives on their arms; sailors of) Tj T*
(Provence, brown and eloquent; thick-set Basques and fair Flemings--all) Tj T*
(these men whom I have commanded, managed, loved, hasten along their) Tj T*
(way. A kind of enchantment dilates their eyes, a sort of innocent) Tj T*
(ecstasy. They go gaily towards the sea and the combat, towards their) Tj T*
(constant mistress and their unknown bride. Already the squadrons have) Tj T*
(steam up; a forest of stacks vomit streamers of smoke which portend) Tj T*
(adventurous cruises. They get under weigh to-night; perhaps to-morrow) Tj T*
(the great adventure will occur. The sides of the ironclads and cruisers) Tj T*
(in the roadstead let loose a flock of boats and launches to seek on the) Tj T*
(quay their loads of brave marines.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Around the approaches to the wharves it is impossible to move. There) Tj T*
(is a suppressed shuffling of feet; only jackets and uniforms can get) Tj T*
(through to the boats. I slip in. On the sidewalk a Breton woman is) Tj T*
(weeping softly into the corner of her apron; her four little children,) Tj T*
(lost in the forest of legs, press round her skirt, clutching the cloth) Tj T*
(with their fingers; heads turned upward, they watch through great) Tj T*
(limpid eyes the endless flow of people. Each step reveals a similar) Tj T*
(scene; women clasp for the last time their beloved son, lover, or) Tj T*
(husband; their frail arms cannot let him go, and their tongues are) Tj T*
(stammering inexpressible things. Yet, as I listen, I hear in all this) Tj T*
(chorus of despair not a single word of revolt. These women comprehend) Tj T*
(everything. They nod their heads approvingly at the words of those who) Tj T*
(are leaving them. Their last kiss holds even a smile, a heavenly smile,) Tj T*
(which the fighting man is to carry with him on the sea, and recall at) Tj T*
(the instant of death. But when the sailor has disappeared toward the) Tj T*
(boats, the smile slowly fades; the women bite their lips, their faces) Tj T*
(grow distorted, and the tears, more sublime for having been held back) Tj T*
(so long, trickle through lids which for many months will not cease to) Tj T*
(weep.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As becomes naval tragedies, the farewell took place in a splendid) Tj T*
(setting. The twilight was glorious with an incomparable splendor of) Tj T*
(sky, and the purple evening seemed to vibrate in unison with the city.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
