BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(On the edge of the quay, between the boats and the crowd, I could watch) Tj T*
(the faces, both of those who had to stay behind, and of those who were) Tj T*
(leaving. As long as the sailors were forcing their way along between) Tj T*
(those parting embraces and the boats, they were pale beneath their) Tj T*
(tan, and only with difficulty restrained their sobs. But hardly had) Tj T*
(they jumped to the benches of the launches, hardly had their comrades) Tj T*
(greeted them with hearty blows on shoulders and hips, when their color) Tj T*
(returned, their mouths let forth sonorous pleasantries, and they) Tj T*
(thought no more of anything but the sea and the adventure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At my feet hundreds of sailors are laughing and singing; they) Tj T*
(intoxicate themselves with anticipation in order not to betray their) Tj T*
(tenderness and their grief. On the quay, overshadowing this gayety,) Tj T*
(stands a forlorn crowd; those in front smile vaguely, but those) Tj T*
(behind are silently choking back their tears. And over there in the) Tj T*
(gold-flaked roadstead, the gray ships sparkle in the setting sun. All) Tj T*
(faces turn towards them. They are the geniuses of the moment. Entrusted) Tj T*
(with a portion of the honor of France, they await their orders. Before) Tj T*
(their prows, the country has swung open the gates of glory. Their guns) Tj T*
(and their sailors are made of the same steel.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                                                             _3 August._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the morning I went with some friends to the top of Cape Capet to see) Tj T*
(the departure of the ?naval army.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the _Courbet_, flagship of the commander-in-chief, the admirals) Tj T*
(had assembled in a night council of war. A few hours later, in the) Tj T*
(deep silence of the blue morning, the squadrons began to move. One) Tj T*
(after another, they took position and formed before our eyes; we) Tj T*
(heard the faint sound of orders. On the heavy water the ships moved) Tj T*
(without an eddy; squat, slender, or graceful, battleships, cruisers,) Tj T*
(or torpedo-boats fell into well-ordered formations, and quietly took) Tj T*
(their proper distances and intervals. They reminded one of ancient) Tj T*
(gladiators stripped naked for the combat. During these last days they) Tj T*
(had sent back to the land-stores all the superfluities of peace; they) Tj T*
(had kept only the bare necessities in rigging and boats, and the paint) Tj T*
(on their steel sides had disappeared under the hand of the scraper.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Their only ornament is the curling smoke which rises through the still) Tj T*
(air and mingles on high in an immense cloud modeled by the faint) Tj T*
(breeze. Their only paint is the light flashing on port-holes and brass.) Tj T*
(Their only finery, the guns, well-cleared, with mouths pointing out to) Tj T*
(sea. They are beautiful and they are invincible. Designed for battle) Tj T*
(and the chase, they push their bows through the water they know so) Tj T*
(well, on their way to carry to enemy shores the frontier of France. At) Tj T*
(the hour when human beings are still asleep, they go to take possession) Tj T*
(of the field of battle.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Their task is various and hard, and without any doubt destined to) Tj T*
(remain unappreciated. On the sea the paths are innumerable, and the) Tj T*
ET
