(have hoisted the flag of battle; and the _Waldeck-Rousseau_, springing) Tj T*
(over the water like a full-blooded steed, leads the cruisers and the) Tj T*
(two squadrons to the adventure where death awaits.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A few minutes of anxious silence pass. Shut in the cells below, the) Tj T*
(men listen, trying to catch the heavy sound of the first broadside;) Tj T*
(they would be killed in an instant if a well-aimed torpedo should touch) Tj T*
(the cruiser, but they devote their stalwart souls to the machinery) Tj T*
(and the engines, that no one may be wanting in this crisis. Through) Tj T*
(their telescopes the gun-pointers watch the distance vanishing as if) Tj T*
(by miracle. Twenty thousand meters.... Eighteen thousand.... Fifteen) Tj T*
(thousand.... Fourteen thousand.... Only two thousand more, and the) Tj T*
(rattle of our artillery will rain upon the enemy. In three parallel) Tj T*
(lines the Austrian destroyers throw out torrents of smoke, which seem) Tj T*
(to merge; each line glides over the blue water like a shining serpent.) Tj T*
(Around us our own destroyers have closed up, and are plowing up clods) Tj T*
(of spray silvered by the sunlight.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But what is this! The Austrian lines swerve, deflect; their head makes) Tj T*
(a great curve! Is it possible? They would retreat! They would refuse) Tj T*
(an engagement! We are so angry that our eyes refuse to believe the) Tj T*
(retreat. It is an illusion of the light; a jest of the wind that makes) Tj T*
(the smoke bend. Not at all. They complete the circle, turn their backs) Tj T*
(on us, and fly off at top speed like three trains along their rails of) Tj T*
(foam.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Oh! To have this revenge in sight for so many futile weeks, and then) Tj T*
(to see it escape just at the point where our guns cannot reach! To) Tj T*
(feel that the great engines under our feet, strong as they are, are) Tj T*
(unable to catch the prey, because its legs are too long! To measure) Tj T*
(the distance, and feel it increase a little with every second, like an) Tj T*
(elastic band of air stretched between us! Fourteen thousand meters!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Fourteen thousand one hundred.... Fourteen thousand two hundred. Ah! we) Tj T*
(should like to be able to control the waves, to throw into the air a) Tj T*
(sudden squall, to chop up a sea of billows and swells. Our own powerful) Tj T*
(keels would not be slowed down, but the destroyers would run foul of) Tj T*
(each crest of the waves, would slacken, become exhausted, and our) Tj T*
(mettle would triumph over their cowardice.) Tj T*
