() Tj T*
(Further out, further north, the cruisers scout between Cattaro and the) Tj T*
(menaced harbors. Towards the end of one of their Ionian cruises, the) Tj T*
(Commander-in-Chief designates them in succession for this work; they do) Tj T*
(not care for these expeditions in which there are blows to receive, but) Tj T*
(none to give.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For from beginning to end of these voyages to succor Montenegro danger) Tj T*
(is present. Every time the cruisers ascend the Adriatic, they meet with) Tj T*
(mines set loose by the Austrians, which trail adrift, like evil beasts) Tj T*
(too lazy to run after their prey. On our last cruise the _Waldeck_) Tj T*
(and her comrade saw several, though the brown speck is scarcely) Tj T*
(distinguishable upon the blue carpet of the waves. We approached them) Tj T*
(at a respectful distance, for fear they might be bound together like) Tj T*
(beads, and that other mines, invisible, would hit us as we passed. We) Tj T*
(demolished them with gunfire, without halting; they burst and sank,) Tj T*
(throwing off an inoffensive sheaf of yellow smoke. But what will happen) Tj T*
(to the ship that strikes one of them in the thick of the darkness,) Tj T*
(where one sees nothing?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And then, as the cruisers do every time, we watched all night the) Tj T*
(approaches of Cattaro, ready to receive the submarines and destroyers) Tj T*
(which will not fail sooner or later to attack the supply-boats at) Tj T*
(Antivari or Medua. In the misty, rainy atmosphere the searchlights of) Tj T*
(the Austrian harbor flash and sweep the sea, searching for the enemy) Tj T*
(they suspect to be within range. These great white restless eyes wander) Tj T*
(ceaselessly to right and left, and halt sometimes upon us. But the) Tj T*
(cruisers are so far away, shrouded in rain, that the enemy confuses) Tj T*
(them with the sheets of rain that are falling. Behind their guns,) Tj T*
(lighted up like specters, our gunners hold themselves ready to reply to) Tj T*
(the fire of the batteries if they begin. But nothing happens except a) Tj T*
(silent waiting; the searchlights abandon our vague outline, and go on) Tj T*
(indefinitely sweeping space.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From hour to hour our anxious wireless asks news of the unloading. And) Tj T*
(the destroyer anchored near the cargo-boat answers in short phrases:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Rain and hail. Half done.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Or else:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Very bad weather. We do not expect to be through before six in the) Tj T*
(morning.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As night wears away, thanks to the tension of mind and the bad weather,) Tj T*
(disquietude increases on the cruisers. They would like to be nearer,) Tj T*
(sharing the danger of the others. They fear some catastrophe before) Tj T*
(dawn.) Tj T*
