(offices were thinking only of the glory of their navy, of the fate of) Tj T*
(their country. Would these same men, as officers of the watch, hearing) Tj T*
(the appeal of drowning men, have the terrible courage to flee?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(During the long hours of the watch I have pondered over this riddle.) Tj T*
(To-morrow, this evening, in an instant, the drama of which I am) Tj T*
(thinking may rise on the horizon. If fate wills that I be struck, I) Tj T*
(know that as my mouth fills with water, my last cry, to those who) Tj T*
(approach me will be this:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Begone, for the submarine is watching for you too!?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But if in the treacherous night or under the kindly sun, I see one of) Tj T*
(the companion ships of the _Waldeck-Rousseau_ destroyed, I hope some) Tj T*
(inspiration will dictate my conduct. I cannot foresee what it will be.) Tj T*
(There are tragedies where the reason stumbles, and man is outdone by) Tj T*
(the malignity of things, where only revelation and divine grace permit) Tj T*
(him to find his way.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
