(them in soft colors; April envelops them in a virginal light. At) Tj T*
(the end of Italy the lighthouse of Leuca rises like a marble finger) Tj T*
(always white; and the islands and the mountains of Epirus are pink in) Tj T*
(the morning, blue through the day, and mauve at dusk. The air is so) Tj T*
(marvelously pure that the night itself does not rob things of their) Tj T*
(color. Violets and yellows remain, even under the moon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We have plenty of time to admire these beauties, already so familiar.) Tj T*
(The cruisers move very slowly, for they must not use too much coal.) Tj T*
(Several times we have been surprised to find one of them wanting to) Tj T*
(hurry the schedule of coalings and do its provisioning two or three) Tj T*
(days earlier. So in order not to incur the reproach of stopping oftener) Tj T*
(than is necessary, the cruisers have taken a leisurely pace, and the) Tj T*
(consumption of fuel has become satisfactory. Especially at night, in) Tj T*
(the religious calm over which the hills of Corfu and the lighthouse of) Tj T*
(Leuca watch as sentinels, it seems that we are quite motionless.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The family of cruisers, which was formerly dispersed over the Ionian) Tj T*
(Sea, now meet each other continually, and play at puss in the corner.) Tj T*
