(did not suspect that this moment, which followed so many other moments,) Tj T*
(held in it the last breath they would draw.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then a dull sound behind her made the cruiser tremble. She was seized) Tj T*
(with a sudden fever, and each of her metal plates resounded. Death) Tj T*
(spread through her limbs and muscles. In through a breach in her very) Tj T*
(heart rushed the dark water, leaped and broke everything before it.) Tj T*
(What happened then?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I do not know. I do not yet know. But some of the messages make it) Tj T*
(possible to imagine the details of the horror.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Filled with water on her wounded side, the _Gambetta_, lurched toward) Tj T*
(her sea grave, and the sailors who were not killed at once thrust) Tj T*
(out their arms to save themselves. Everything slid around them. To) Tj T*
(stand upright they had to lean over; their hands had to serve them) Tj T*
(for eyes, for darkness enveloped the cruiser. Naked and silent they) Tj T*
(rushed on toward the deck, but the slanting companion ways were now) Tj T*
(as perpendicular as walls. How many unfortunates perished in their) Tj T*
(suddenly interrupted sleep, without realizing that their ship was going) Tj T*
(down for the last time?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the deck, a black chaos! Each second the cruiser sinks deeper.) Tj T*
(The gulf of the waves grows larger, and each moment perhaps will be) Tj T*
(the final plunge. By main force the sailors launch the boats and the) Tj T*
(cutter, which drop into the water wrong side or right side up. The) Tj T*
(officers are calm and have put aside their fatigue; they give the) Tj T*
(necessary orders for the rescue. In the sky the two masts and the four) Tj T*
(stacks sink lower and lower. The cruiser, with its apparatus damaged,) Tj T*
(can send out no signal for help, and all those who dwell on her plunge) Tj T*
(into the depths as if down a silent stair.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A handful of men have been able to enter the boats. Chilled, but) Tj T*
(struggling for life, they have taken the oars, and during the last) Tj T*
(hours of the night have rowed towards the friendly lighthouse. At) Tj T*
(the first gleam of day, with bleeding hands, but with a marvelous) Tj T*
(tenacity of will, they have made a supreme effort, and the Italian) Tj T*
(customs-officers take in sixty exhausted men almost at the point of) Tj T*
(death.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From Tarentum to Rome, from Rome to Paris, from Paris to Malta, and) Tj T*
(from Malta to the _Waldeck-Rousseau_, this story of the drama has been) Tj T*
(traveling for twenty-four hours. The good neighbor we loved to see) Tj T*
(in our meetings on the high sea has met the death which might have) Tj T*
(been our own. She has disappeared without a word, felled at the first) Tj T*
(stroke in an eddy of the sea, as befalls her pilgrims. The wound was) Tj T*
(muffled and dumb, for over there on the horizon I saw nothing. One of) Tj T*
(the flashes that played in the sky was perhaps the gleam of the torpedo) Tj T*
